99
00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:01.120
and you're facing the pain of that already, right?

100
00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.280
So how does this sprawl manifest actually,

101
00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:08.200
and impact humans and the agents that we're instructing.

102
00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.000
So there's a couple of different failure modes that we're seeing.

103
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.480
The first one is overlap.

104
00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:18.040
So this is where you might have multiple teams all building the same thing

105
00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:22.240
in isolation, without realizing each team might have built their own version

106
00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:25.040
of the skill. It might achieve the same thing.

107
00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:25.520
You might have

108
00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.520
generally the same outcomes, but it's done in a different way, right?

109
00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:31.520
And you're having multiple kind of wasted effort there.

110
00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:32.840
So that's overlap.

111
00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.800
Next up we often see drift.

112
00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.920
So this is where newer skills are shipping.

113
00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.280
And teams aren't keeping up with those newer versions.

114
00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.920
And a good example here recently is Matt Pocock.

115
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:47.040
Hopefully you've all heard of him.

116
00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.960
He's great educator, published a bunch of great skills.

117
00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:51.880
He has a grill me skill.

118
00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:55.600
That's where you kind of do an interview with the agent and you kind of

119
00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:56.880
get a shared understanding.

120
00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.480
He recently published a newer version of that grill school called grill

121
00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.880
with docs, which kind of expands on it and adds a lot more detail to it.

122
00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:06.800
I'm sure there's lots of folks that don't realize that that's been released, right?

123
00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.920
And they're still using that order version and haven't updated.

124
00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:11.680
So that's drift.

125
00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:16.840
We also have problems around activation or a lack of activation, right.

126
00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.440
Lots of people are producing these skills.

127
00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:19.880
Are they being used by the agents.

128
00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:21.480
Are they being used by humans.

129
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:24.120
There's no real way to to understand that

130
00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:27.120
and know that right now you probably don't have that visibility yet.

131
00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:30.760
So Guy mentioned this in his talk as well.

132
00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:31.920
This morning.

133
00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.360
You might have a skill that describes some code or a code base, or a process

134
00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:41.120
or even a workflow, and those two things can quickly go out of sync

135
00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.440
so your skill never gets updated. Things change.

136
00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:47.360
And in those instances, having an outdated skill can often

137
00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:50.680
be just as bad as having no skill at all.

138
00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:51.760
Right?

139
00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:55.280
Next up is overloading

140
00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:58.760
the last one here, and this is one that you might not be aware of.

141
00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.320
This is having too many skills in a single repository.

142
00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:06.400
So right now you might not know, but there is actually a limit

143
00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.840
to the amount of skills you can have with most coding agents,

144
00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:13.680
often expressed as a percentage of the context window.

145
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:17.160
So if we think about our skills, the name and the description gets injected

146
00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:20.960
into the start of every context window, as the agent knows about those skills

147
00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:25.880
and knows when to use them, makes decision about activating them.

148
00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.440
What can happen here is undefined.

149
00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:32.320
Most agents will truncate that description,

150
00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:36.640
which means you end up in a situation where your skills might not be activated

151
00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:37.960
because the description

152
00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:40.080
has been shortened, and key bits of information

153
00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:44.040
that the model kind of needs to know about to be able to to invoke that skill

154
00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.840
and not there anymore. Right.

155
00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:48.840
So these are the the major failure modes that we're seeing.

156
00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:54.120
So we set out to detect and actually solve these problems for our customers.

157
00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:57.280
And so we've built a skills of entry right.

158
00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.520
We connect to your GitHub org.

159
00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.920
We pull in all the skills we find across all your repos.

160
00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:05.560
We let you kind of drill into how they're being used.

161
00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:07.280
So you can break it down by repo.

162
00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:08.880
You can break it down by skill.

163
00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.960
You can see those first party skills, those third party skills.

164
00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:14.640
You can see token usage on those skills.

165
00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.800
And we are also able to surface those findings as well that it talks about.

166
00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:22.760
So that previous slide with those different failure modes

167
00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:25.760
we're able to find those surfaced them to you produce a report

168
00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:28.560
and then you can go and fix that and decide what you want to do.

169
00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:31.960
We're also able to lean on our public registry.

170
00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:33.680
And if we're finding a third party

171
00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.800
skill that you're using within, there will also surface any reviews

172
00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:41.720
or eval scores or security scans that we've also done on that skill.

173
00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.360
This is currently in private beta.

174
00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.400
It's very, very new.

175
00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:46.920
If you're interested, come and find me.

176
00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.560
I'm working on the Tessl booth for the rest of the day.

177
00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.600
I'm more than happy to chat and give you a quick demo.

178
00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:56.200
Run you through this if you're having the same problems as most people seem to be.

179
00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:00.080
So quick recap on Sproule.

180
00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:01.720
Let's go.

181
00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:02.080
Right.

182
00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:05.120
We need to start measuring how we're actually using skills

183
00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:06.600
across our organization.

184
00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:08.280
And we need to track that over time

185
00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.760
so that we can improve that and resolve those issues.

186
00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:14.600
So that's a recap.

187
00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.560
Next up I want to talk about skills and software.

188
00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:19.240
So this is kind of building on a lot of the things

189
00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:20.600
that Guy talked about this morning.

190
00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:24.240
I want to give you some actionable things that you can take away and use

191
00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.680
within your own teams and your own organizations today.

192
00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:31.000
So I'm not going to go into lots of detail

193
00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:34.280
about what makes an individual skill work.

194
00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:37.640
Hopefully many of you in here are already familiar with that.

195
00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:41.120
There's lots of resources on there, but I want to talk about the kind of ecosystem

196
00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:44.120
of exists around skills.

197
00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:48.680
Before we dive into that, I want to talk about the agent equation.

198
00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:49.040
Right.

199
00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:53.000
So if we think about it, an agent is a function of the model,

200
00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:55.560
the harness and the context. Right.

201
00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:58.560
Changing any one of these three pieces has an impact

202
00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.880
on how the agent works and its output, and the quality of its output.

203
00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:02.840
Right.

204
00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:06.600
Most of the time, we're probably not going to change the model of the harness

205
00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.320
so much that's harder to do.

206
00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:11.080
Or maybe we're kind of tied into using a certain model

207
00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:13.760
or a certain harness within the organization we're working in.

208
00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:17.040
And so context is effectively the best lever

209
00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.760
that we can pull to affect the outcome of the agent here.

210
00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.960
And it has a pretty big impact as well.

211
00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.400
Right. I'm sure most of you are familiar with this, right.

212
00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:30.240
You can do a lot in terms of where you're doing instructions and the skills you use

213
00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:33.240
and the prompting you use, and the kind of hooks you have set up.

214
00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:35.880
So within context, skills, again,

215
00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.800
feel like an important kind of powerful piece

216
00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:42.800
because they can encode business or domain specific logic

217
00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:44.400
and that the model won't have been trained on.

218
00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.920
So you can give the model that knowledge that it won't otherwise know about.

219
00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:50.920
So given all of this thinking about this, what can we do

220
00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:53.920
to make skills as effective as possible?

221
00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:56.040
So first one nice and easy.

222
00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:58.200
Hopefully you're already doing this.

223
00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:00.320
Don't write monolithic skills right.

224
00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:02.800
Don't write huge epic skills.

225
00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.440
Think about decomposing breaking skills down into smaller pieces.

226
00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:09.320
Most agent coding tools support the use of plugins.

227
00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:12.160
If you're

228
00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:15.880
not familiar with plugins, plugin is just a group of context

229
00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:19.440
related together that can be installed into your agent.

230
00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:20.800
Coding tools.

231
00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:25.440
Agents can then pick and choose which parts of the plugins they want to use.

232
00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:28.480
Because you're breaking down your skill into smaller ones,

233
00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:32.440
you can use descriptions for each of those individual pieces,

234
00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:34.320
and so the agent is more likely to activate

235
00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.680
those individually, rather than trying to stuff a load of

236
00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:40.000
kind of keywords into a bigger description of a larger skill.

237
00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:43.040
And you can also end up building

238
00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:46.120
kind of pretty complex, interconnected skills as well.

239
00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:49.040
So I mentioned this morning that skills can call skills.

240
00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:53.680
And so you can have one skill that is maybe invoked by a human

241
00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:56.760
that sets up the agent to call a bunch of other skills.

242
00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:00.960
An example of that recently is we built a UI skill, a test

243
00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:05.240
which enabled us to build user interfaces in the browser.

244
00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:08.000
So we would take a Figma design.

245
00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:11.240
We would feed that into one skill that was able to break that Figma design

246
00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:14.440
down into its various components, and then hand that off to somebody

247
00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:16.360
using other skills to implement those.

248
00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:20.240
So we had a specific skill for building kind of low level components like buttons,

249
00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:24.120
a separate one for building sort of high level components,

250
00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:27.240
things like pages, and another one for those things in the middle,

251
00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:31.200
things like forms that perhaps compose buttons, text inputs, that kind of thing.

